IMPORT prelude

§ `Set operators — Phase 2: the overloads, and the two ANDs side by side`

-- The acceptance test of SET-OPERATORS-SPEC.md §5: the same token AND,
-- disambiguated by type. Term-level AND on SETs is the lattice join (union);
-- sentence-level AND on BOOLEANs stays conjunction. Partee & Rooth (1983),
-- implemented.

§§ `Term-level AND: union`

`new yorkers`   MEANS setFromList (LIST "alice", "bob")
`new jerseyans` MEANS setFromList (LIST "carol")

-- "residents of New York and New Jersey may apply": either state qualifies.
`eligible` MEANS `new yorkers` AND `new jerseyans`

#EVAL setSize `eligible`
#EVAL `is in` "carol" `eligible`

-- OR on sets is the same operation — the collapse the lint will surface.
#EVAL setSize (`new yorkers` OR `new jerseyans`)

§§ `Sentence-level AND: still conjunction`

`is cruel`   MEANS TRUE
`is unusual` MEANS FALSE

-- "cruel and unusual punishments": must be both.
`violates 8th amendment` MEANS `is cruel` AND `is unusual`

#EVAL `violates 8th amendment`

§§ `PLUS and MINUS: bare, precedence-correct — and not a group`

a1 MEANS setFromList (LIST 1, 2, 3)
b1 MEANS setFromList (LIST 2, 3, 4)

#EVAL setSize (a1 PLUS b1)
#EVAL setSize (a1 MINUS b1)
-- unparenthesized chain: left-associates at arithmetic precedence
#EVAL setSize (a1 PLUS b1 MINUS b1)
-- the semilattice trap, demonstrated: (a1 PLUS b1) MINUS b1 is NOT a1
#EVAL setToList (a1 PLUS b1 MINUS b1)
-- numeric dispatch is untouched
#EVAL 5 MINUS 2
#EVAL 5 PLUS 2

§§ `Extensional equality`

-- order and duplicates are invisible under `set equals`.
-- (Bare EQUALS on two SETs is deliberately an ambiguity error — see
-- not-ok/tc/set-equals-ambiguous.l4 and prelude `Sets` notes.)
#EVAL `set equals` (setFromList (LIST 2, 1, 1)) (setFromList (LIST 1, 2))
#EVAL `set equals` (setFromList (LIST 1)) (setFromList (LIST 1, 2))
-- numeric and string EQUALS untouched
#EVAL 42 EQUALS 42
#EVAL "a" EQUALS "b"
